From 642f04773db65ff8cba499191c1b4355718e1d71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 1 Jul 2026 11:50:28 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05301/11362] [outer-loop] exp5108: KAN-PWA/MILP scale stress
 test finds the wall at N=10-20

Operator: 'can we tackle this in the outer loop instead of waiting for the
conductor' -> yes. Answers the one question 6+ weeks of the exp2051..exp5098
KAN-PWA/MILP lineage never tested: does exact-MILP verification of additive
KAEM energy bounds scale toward the real production reference (N=100, the
documented low-rank/full-rank KAEM cutover in verify_repair.py,
REQ-SAMPLE-029)?

Reused the exp5091/5098 encoding unchanged (same MILP construction, same
adversarial-rigor pattern: true property + engineered-false control +
margin-abstention), generalized to N units with a wall-clock solver timeout
(Z3 native timeout param) and one solve per N instead of 3 redundant solves
(documented efficiency change -- the solver's exact max is identical
regardless of which threshold it's later compared against, so no rigor lost).

RESULT: N=5 solved in 0.15s; N=10 solved in 120.9s (~800x jump for 2x the
units); N=20 timed out at 300s. The wall sits between N=10 and N=20 -- one
order of magnitude below the N=100 production target. Binary-variable/
constraint counts scaled perfectly linearly (3N / ~21N+1) while solve time
exploded combinatorially -- the textbook MILP NP-hardness signature.
Adversarial rigor (false-control + margin-abstention) held at every N that
solved.

HONEST CONCLUSION: this exact-MILP approach does not currently scale to
Carnot's deployed KAEM configuration. Closes the question this lineage spent
6+ weeks not asking; future formal-verification work on KAN energy models
should pursue abstraction-refinement or sampling-bound alternatives instead
of more toy-scale MILP attempts.

Also: openspec/capabilities/kan/spec.md gets REQ-KAN-5108/SCENARIO-KAN-5108
(Spec First discipline), tests/python/test_experiment_5108_...py (7 tests,
all pass, including a monkeypatched deterministic timeout-stop test rather
than racing real Z3 solve times), and ops/known-issues.md records the
resolved finding (closing the task drafted earlier this session).

NOTE: the initial (120s-timeout, N=5/10 only) draft of this file was
independently swept into the conductor's '.468 operational retrospective'
checkpoint commit (45fb0d87a) via its standard 'preserve uncommitted work'
git-add -A behavior on the shared working directory -- confirmed byte-
identical to my own first sweep, not independent conductor research. This
commit supersedes that checkpoint with the completed, refined version.

